 "discussion": "* meets , with numerous reviews of his books, also meets  in his field, Seljuq dynasty [[User:Coolabahapple]] 03:46, 3 June 2018 (UTC)\n* With the additional books now listed and the reviews I found for them, our article now lists three single-author books, one multi-author book, and four edited volumes, with a total of 16 published reviews. That's enough for  and probably also [[User:David]] 17:54, 31 May 2018 (UTC)\n* \"The subject is verifably Professor of Middle Eastern and Islamic History at University of St Andrews, with books published by Oxford University Press https://global.oup.com/academic/product/the-frontiers-of-the-ottoman-world-9780197264423?q=A%20C%20S%20Peacock&lang=en&cc=gb, in the Proceedings of the British Academy https://global.oup.com/academic/product/from-anatolia-to-aceh-9780197265819?cc=gb&lang=en&, and by Edinburgh University Press https://edinburghuniversitypress.com/book-the-great-seljuk-empire.html. His field of expertise is rather specialist but his publications are reviewed within their field. See the several journal reviews references in the article. And although the full article is paywalled, the portion of Jürgen Paul's Journal of Islamic Studies article which is visible https://academic.oup.com/jis/article-abstract/28/2/237/2967621 here includes this assessment: ''\"\"...Peacock’s two volumes form the actual works of reference for the Seljuq dynasty and Iranian (and partly Syrian and Caucasian) societies in the eleventh and twelfth centuries and constitute a new point of departure for the ongoing re-evaluation of this crucial period in the history of the Middle East. This process has been going on for the last decade or so, and Andrew Peacock has been one of the driving forces...\"\"''. That is indicative of  criterion 1 and I think criteria 3 and 5 likely to be met by virtue of the factors which I discussed above\" [[User:AllyD]] 19:32, 30 May 2018 (UTC)\n* meets  with substantive reviews of the subject's works [[User:K]] 20:19, 2 June 2018 (UTC)",
