{
 "page": "Articles for deletion/Thomas E. Caldecott",
 "discussion": "* Article is sourced properly and satisfies [[User:Shoessss]] 10:09, 22 March 2019 (UTC)\n* \"I have added more sources (yes, newspapers, as well as a book). There are plenty of secondary sources, and it is clear from articles in 1937, 1951 and 1960 that he was acknowledged as \"\"the man responsible for the success\"\" of the Broadway Low Level Tunnel project. It was a major project, with a very large cost for the time, and the original contractors were unable to complete it so it had to be retendered - this is in the sources I have added, though I did not add that information to this article, assuming it would be in the tunnel article (though I see that it's not). That is why he is described as having fought for it, championed it, put untiring effort into it, etc. I don't have access to all newspapers, eg not the ''Berkeley Gazette'', otherwise I would add inline citations to the obituary it published. I think there is enough for him to meet , but if the article is not kept, I would recommend merging to Caldecott Tunnel, so that his role in its construction is recognised\" [[User:RebeccaGreen]] 14:00, 5 April 2019 (UTC)\n* \"The highest office this person held is mayor of a midsized city — which means that he could have an article if he could be referenced well enough to clear  #2 (\"\"major local political figures who have received significant press coverage\"\"), but it isn't a strong enough notability claim to guarantee him an article in the ''absence'' of solid sourcing. Mayors are no longer automatically presumed notable just because the city they were mayor of eventually surpassed an arbitrary population cutoff — the notability test for a mayor is the ability to write and source a substantive article about his political ''impact'', not just the ability to verify the names of his wife and kids, so one obituary in the local newspaper upon his death is not enough sourcing to get a mayor over the bar all by itself\" [[User:Bearcat]] 19:03, 22 March 2019 (UTC)\n* in a matter of minutes I managed to drum up two interesting sources for this individual--not bad for someone born in 1878.  There may be some editing issues that need to take place, but I see no reason not to |assume good faith that the content of the article is accurate at this point.  It appears to be an article worth some attention and the individual seems to have had an impact on politics and the roads in California.  Notability speaks to  and the breadth of independent sources have provided significant coverage of the individual, in my eyes enough to pass  so there it is [[User:Paulmcdonald]] 19:52, 26 March 2019 (UTC)",
 "participants": "4 editors"
}

{
 "page": "Articles for deletion/List of Michael Bloomberg 2020 presidential campaign endorsements",
 "discussion": "* Valid split, , and https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?sort=relevance&search=%22presidential+campaign+endorsements%22&title=Special:Search&profile=advanced&fulltext=1&advancedSearch-current=%7B%7D&ns4=1&ns5=1&searchToken=cdeuwl6ulta8ylqf7l8q1217uhttps://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Special:Search&limit=500&offset=0&profile=default&search=%22presidential+campaign+endorsements%22&advancedSearch-current={}&ns0=1 mean that there's more than enough precedent that individual AFDs are inappropriate, but also more than enough precedent to suggest that an RFC on removal of endorsements in toto is almost certain to fail (the case for retention is stronger than [[User:Hydronium]] 08:30, 29 February 2020 (UTC)\n* \"Probably more than most, Bloomberg's endorsement list is independently notable. This was something more reflected in Special:Permalink/941802927|this version of the article, but that was Special:Diff/941803219|moved a while ago to the campaign page where it was just removed as Special:Diff/943380878|\"\"vandalism\"\".I was considering a  close for this discussion, but I chatted a bit  with {{u|Deacon Vorbis}} on iscord about that possibility before doing so (wanting to avoid a ELREV). As it stands with , editors generally want to see coverage roughly proportional to a candidate's support. Is that fair? No, probably not. It doesn't really work well with , but for editors in this area it is one of the few things that manage to keep the peace amongst participants. If you want to see the inevitable fall out where one article is created about a candidate (for notability reasons) and |Other stuff doesn't exist, see Wikipedia:Articles for deletion/Media bias against Bernie Sanders|AFD:Media bias against Bernie Sanders.As I said on Discord, the best bet is to withdraw and do a bundled nomination. Alternatively, I would recommend another test case that isn't as covered as much in media as this one; like List of Cory Booker 2020 presidential campaign endorsements because editors are *surprisingly* less vocal about keeping around stuff about inactive candidates who didn't make a large splash (see all the since redirected campaign pages). &#8211\" [[User:MJL]] 16:18, 1 March 2020 (UTC)\n* \"This list and its topic are independently notable because its group of notable Bloomberg endorsers is tracked and discussed by independent reliable sources like Fivethirtyeight.  See .  Merge with Endorsements in the 2020 Democratic Party presidential primaries is not appropriate here because Bloomberg's list is notable and long enough to justify a stand-alone list, unlike John Delaney. Per , endorsements lists are limited by only including entries for independently notable endorsers or those with Wikipedia articles when the entries are supported by independent reliable sources and use the word \"\"endorse\"\" or synonymous language. The criteria ensure that endorsement lists are groups of notable endorsers and not indiscriminate catalogs of non-notable supporters.  The value of notable endorsement lists is discussed in Wikipedia:Articles for deletion/Congressional endorsements for the 2008 presidential election, Wikipedia:Articles for deletion/List of Hillary Clinton presidential campaign non-political endorsements, 2016, and Wikipedia:Articles for deletion/List of Bernie Sanders presidential campaign endorsements, 2016. Deletion of all articles in :Category:United States presidential election endorsements seems unreasonable, especially now that editors have developed strict criteria for endorsement lists in \" [[User:Tvc]] 22:14, 2 March 2020 (UTC)",
 "participants": "3 editors"
}

